WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.380 --> 00:00:10.210
Mike: One of the things we commonly, as security
consultants, train developments teams for-

00:00:10.210 --> 00:00:14.830
and architects and other folks in the creation
of software- is threat modeling.

00:00:14.830 --> 00:00:19.919
And, that's where we kind of just go through
a brainstorming exercise and we think of all

00:00:19.919 --> 00:00:24.880
the threats that exist against what we're
trying to create, and think about how the

00:00:24.880 --> 00:00:26.730
application can be attacked.

00:00:26.730 --> 00:00:28.289
How can it be compromised?

00:00:28.289 --> 00:00:32.940
And if these attacks are successful, what
does it mean against the bottom line?

00:00:32.940 --> 00:00:40.300
How much is it going to cost us if a certain
attack results in a breach of some sort of

00:00:40.300 --> 00:00:41.300
data?

00:00:41.300 --> 00:00:45.750
Pete: So, that's threat modeling and there's
also the concept of modeling out abuse cases

00:00:45.750 --> 00:00:46.750
as well, right?

00:00:46.750 --> 00:00:47.810
Not just a use case, but-

00:00:47.810 --> 00:00:48.810
Mike: Right.

00:00:48.810 --> 00:00:49.810
An abuse case.

00:00:49.810 --> 00:00:51.420
Pete: Many developers don't think in terms
of an abuse case.

00:00:51.420 --> 00:00:54.640
What would a malicious person, someone with
malicious intent, do?

00:00:54.640 --> 00:00:58.650
How would they walk through the application,
how could they potentially turn it on itself.

00:00:58.650 --> 00:00:59.650
Mike: Right.

00:00:59.650 --> 00:01:05.059
So, a use case might be- we need to let the
user enter their credentials and store them

00:01:05.059 --> 00:01:08.030
permanently or semi-permanently.

00:01:08.030 --> 00:01:15.409
An abuse case for that would be- well, a third
party application exists on the device, the

00:01:15.409 --> 00:01:21.530
device is jailbroken and it spiders the filesystem
and finds this file containing our user's

00:01:21.530 --> 00:01:22.530
credentials.

00:01:22.530 --> 00:01:26.520
Pete: Or an attacker trying to abuse the forgot
password feature to try to gain access to

00:01:26.520 --> 00:01:31.490
an account in an unauthorized way.

00:01:31.490 --> 00:01:33.149
Mike: Exactly.

00:01:33.149 --> 00:01:37.549
So, the point being- we do the threat modeling,
as known as, like a risk assessment.

00:01:37.549 --> 00:01:39.820
We figure out- OK, what is our true risk here?

00:01:39.820 --> 00:01:41.829
Then, we can kind of make a decision.

00:01:41.829 --> 00:01:47.689
Like, OK, maybe it's OK to store these credentials,
it's just for our gaming platform.

00:01:47.689 --> 00:01:53.789
But, when we say, OK, this is an ecommerce
site, and the credentials can be used to potentially

00:01:53.789 --> 00:01:58.499
access credit card data for our customer,
maybe it's not worth it for us to store these

00:01:58.499 --> 00:01:59.590
credentials on the device.

00:01:59.590 --> 00:02:04.851
So, I really think that it's important that
developers and other folks involved in the

00:02:04.851 --> 00:02:09.590
creating of software, they really think about-
what does the application need to do, and

00:02:09.590 --> 00:02:11.800
what are the risks inherent with that?

00:02:11.800 --> 00:02:14.590
And then if they decide, you know what?

00:02:14.590 --> 00:02:16.170
The risk is acceptable to us.

00:02:16.170 --> 00:02:19.030
We'd like to store the credentials on the
device.

00:02:19.030 --> 00:02:24.830
Then, they need to understand what secure
mechanisms exist on the platform that will

00:02:24.830 --> 00:02:26.450
allow them to do that.

00:02:26.450 --> 00:02:27.780
Pete: And this would be platform specific,
right?

00:02:27.780 --> 00:02:28.780
Mike: Platform specific, correct.

00:02:28.780 --> 00:02:32.610
Pete: Well, if I'm a developer and I'm trying
to get used to this concept.

00:02:32.610 --> 00:02:38.490
Maybe this concept of creating abuse cases
is a little bit new to me.

00:02:38.490 --> 00:02:41.010
How do I get started writing an abuse case
for my app?

00:02:41.010 --> 00:02:43.790
I guess basically, think like a hacker.

00:02:43.790 --> 00:02:44.790
Mike: Yea.

00:02:44.790 --> 00:02:50.960
Pete: Any other tips or tricks, in order to
help somebody think about how to come up with

00:02:50.960 --> 00:02:56.070
malicious ways to circumvent auth or do the
nasty things inside an application?

00:02:56.070 --> 00:03:02.050
Mike: Yea, it really is- you have to put your
bad guy hat on and just start to really think

00:03:02.050 --> 00:03:03.290
outside of the box.

00:03:03.290 --> 00:03:06.980
And, that could be pretty hard for you to
do just sitting at your desk by yourself.

00:03:06.980 --> 00:03:12.520
So, it can be a fun exercise to get the whole
team involved and even potentially bring in

00:03:12.520 --> 00:03:14.840
a security guy to just give a talk.

00:03:14.840 --> 00:03:19.490
Or even a little more in depth, a 45 minute
presentation of threat modeling and giving

00:03:19.490 --> 00:03:20.960
you a mock threat model.

00:03:20.960 --> 00:03:30.320
We really open your eyes and get you thinking
in a different way, which is what you need

00:03:30.320 --> 00:03:36.260
to do if you need to kind these flaws before
they come to exist in your app.

